<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amesha Spenta</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ameshaspand</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, the Amesha Spenta, literally 'Immortal (which is) holy', are seven divine entities emanating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a>, through whom, in tradition, all later creation was accomplished. The doctrine is only alluded to in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avesta">Avesta</a> and is set out in later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> texts such as the Bundahishn. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gathas">Gathas</a> each represents a moral quality, such as truth or good thought, that people should strive to acquire, and each has an opposing counterpart. Each is linked to one of the seven creations. The first seven days of the Zoroastrian month are dedicated to them.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The expression Amesha Spenta does not occur in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gathas">Gathas</a>, but it was probably coined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a> himself, since spenta, meaning furthering, strengthening, bounteous or holy, is characteristic of his revelation. Its oldest attested use is in the Yasna Haptanghaiti, where the two elements stand in reverse order, as Spenta Amesha, in a text in Gathic Avestan approximately as old as the hymns attributed to Zoroaster. The doctrine of the seven is only alluded to in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avesta">Avesta</a> and is described systematically in later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> texts, in particular the Bundahishn, an 11th or 12th century work on Zoroastrian cosmology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gathas">Gathas</a> each Amesha Spenta represents a good moral quality that mortals should strive to obtain, so that through good thoughts, words and deeds each person assimilates the qualities of one. The seven are Vohu Manah, Aša Vahišta, Xšaθra Vairya, Armaiti, Haurvatāt and Amərətāt, with Spenta Mainyu, the active principle of creation, counted in the group in the context of cosmology. Each has an opposing counterpart: aša (truth) is opposed to druj (deceit), vohu manah to aka manah, xšaθra to dušaexšaθra, and armaiti to taraemaiti. Haurvatāt (wholeness) is set against thirst and Amərətāt (life) against hunger, oppositions that first appear in the Younger Avesta.</p><p>Aša is the most evident of the seven and the most associated with wisdom: it occurs 157 times in the 238 verses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gathas">Gathas</a>, and vohu manah 136 times. Each of the seven is also linked to one of the seven creations. In the Gathas Xšaθra has no creation of its own, and only later texts make it the guardian of metals; modern scholarship explains this by the earlier belief in a sky of stone, which later became crystal and then metal. Haurvatāt is identified with water and Amərətāt with plants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The word is also used in a non-specific sense for all divinities that further creation, equivalent to yazata; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasna">Yasna</a> 1.2 the yazata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atar">Atar</a> is called the most active of the Amesha Spentas. Today the term often refers to the thirty-three divinities that have a day-name dedication in the calendar or a Yasht.</p><p>Non-Zoroastrian writers attacked the reverence for the Amesha Spenta as polytheism, in the Sassanid era and later. The &quot;worship of the elements&quot; was a repeated accusation in the 4th and 5th centuries, and Christian missionaries in 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, such as John Wilson, targeted the immanence of the Amesha Spenta. Some modern Zoroastrian theologians of the Reformist school hold that spirit and physical manifestation are not separable, so that reverence for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a>'s creations is ultimately worship of the Creator.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amesha Spenta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ahura Mazda works through the <b>Amesha Spentas</b>, the “holy immortals”, divine aspects or beings such as Good Mind, Best Truth and Holy Devotion, each linked to a part of creation, from cattle and fire to earth, water and plants.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
